.TH QT 1
.SH NAME
qt \- Quicktable shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qt
[
.B -d
.I dir
] [
.B -m
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Qt
is an interactive shell on the Mustang wide-column engine.
It opens the store in
.IR dir ,
by default
.BR ./data ,
or with
.B -m
an empty store held in memory and lost on exit.
.PP
A store holds tables, a table holds rows named by a
.IR rowkey ,
and a row holds values named by a
.IR column .
Rows need not share columns,
and a row or table exists only while it holds a value.
Names may not be empty
or contain white space.
.PP
On a terminal,
.I qt
prompts with
.RB \(lq ":\ " \(rq
and reads commands from a line editor.
Up and down step through the history,
control-C discards the line,
and control-D on an empty line quits.
While a batch is open the prompt counts the writes it holds,
as in
.RB \(lq "batch 2:\ " \(rq.
Errors are reported and the shell carries on.
.PP
Otherwise
.I qt
reads one command per line from standard input,
skipping blank lines and lines starting with
.BR # ,
and stops at the first error;
see
.BR DIAGNOSTICS .
.SS Values
A value is a number, string or boolean,
told apart by its spelling:
.TP
.B 30 -1.5 2e3
a number, stored as a 64-bit float.
Only words starting with a digit, sign or point are numbers,
so
.B inf
and
.B nan
are strings.
.TP
.B true false
a boolean.
.TP
.B \(dqtext\(dq
a string.
Any other word is a string too,
but quoting makes one of a word that would be read as another type,
.BR \(dq42\(dq ,
or holds white space,
.BR "\(dqAnn A\(dq" .
Quoted strings take Go backslash escapes.
.PP
There is no time type.
Times written as strings in fixed-width UTC, such as
.BR 2026-11-26T10:00:00Z ,
order as the instants do,
and a shortened time such as
.B 2026-11-26
is a bound for a range.
A year alone is a number; quote it,
.BR \(dq2026\(dq ,
to compare it with times.
.PP
Values are printed in the same form,
strings always quoted,
so anything printed can be typed back.
.SS Reading
.TP
.B tables
Print the name of every table, one per line, sorted.
.TP
.BI rows " table"
Print the key of every row, one per line, sorted.
.TP
.BI cols " table"
Print the name of every column used by any row,
one per line.
This scans every key of the table.
.TP
.BI get " table \fR[\fProwkey \fR[\fPcolumn\fR]]\fP"
Print a whole table, one row or one value.
A row prints as its key followed by
.IB column = value
for each of its columns, sorted.
.TP
.BI column " table column"
Print the key and value of every row that has
.IR column .
.TP
.BI mget " table rowkey column \fR[\fPtable rowkey column ...\fR]\fP"
Print the named values, read from one consistent snapshot,
each after its table, rowkey and column.
A missing value prints as
.BR - .
.PP
On a terminal,
.BR rows ,
.BR cols ,
.B get
and
.B drop
without a table offer the tables to choose from.
.SS Writing
Each write is applied at once,
unless a batch is open.
On a terminal a write prints
.BR ok .
.TP
.BI set " table rowkey column value \fR[\fPcolumn value ...\fR]\fP"
Set one or more values of a row.
.TP
.BI incr " table rowkey column \fR[\fPdelta\fR]\fP"
Add
.IR delta ,
by default 1,
to a number and print the result.
A missing value counts as 0.
Concurrent increments are retried, never lost.
.TP
.BI del " table rowkey \fR[\fPcolumn\fR]\fP"
Delete a row, or one of its values.
Deleting what does not exist is not an error.
.TP
.BI dropcol " table column"
Delete
.I column
from every row of
.IR table .
.TP
.BI drop " table"
Delete
.I table
and all its rows.
On a terminal this asks for confirmation first.
.SS Queries
.TP
.BI query " table \fR[\fPclause ...\fR]\fP"
Print every matching row in key order,
then the number of rows.
.TP
.BI count " table \fR[\fPclause ...\fR]\fP"
Print the number of matching rows.
.TP
.BI sum " table column \fR[\fPclause ...\fR]\fP"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI avg " table column \fR[\fPclause ...\fR]\fP"
.PD
Print the sum or mean of
.I column
over the matching rows that have it.
Its values must all be numbers.
.TP
.BI min " table column \fR[\fPclause ...\fR]\fP"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI max " table column \fR[\fPclause ...\fR]\fP"
.PD
Print the smallest or largest value of
.I column
over the matching rows.
Its values must all be of one type.
.PP
Without clauses every row matches.
The clauses are:
.RS
.TP
.BI prefix " p"
only rows whose key starts with
.IR p .
.TP
.BI from " key"
only rows whose key is at least
.IR key .
.TP
.BI to " key"
only rows whose key is less than
.IR key .
.TP
.BI where " column op \fR[\fPvalue\fR]\fP"
only rows where the test holds;
given more than once, all must hold.
.TP
.BI select " column ..."
print only these columns.
.TP
.BI limit " n"
stop after
.I n
matching rows;
0 means no limit.
.TP
.B reverse
scan keys in descending order,
so
.B limit
keeps the last rows.
.RE
.PP
The operators are
.BR = ,
.BR != ,
.BR < ,
.BR <= ,
.BR > ,
.BR >= ,
.B prefix
and
.B contains
(the value is a string),
and
.B exists
and
.BR missing ,
which take no value.
A value of another type than the operand never matches,
not even
.BR != .
Numbers, strings and booleans order as expected,
strings bytewise.
.SS Batches
.TP
.B begin
Open a batch.
Writes are collected instead of applied,
and
.B incr
prints nothing.
.TP
.BI if " table rowkey column op \fR[\fPvalue\fR]\fP"
Add a condition to the batch,
with
.I op
as in
.BR where .
.TP
.B commit
Apply the batch in one transaction,
conditions and writes in the order they were given.
If a condition fails, a write is invalid,
or a value tested by
.B if
was changed by another writer since,
nothing is written.
.TP
.B abort
Discard the batch.
.PP
Quitting with an open batch asks whether to commit or discard it;
at the end of a script it is discarded with a warning.
.SS Other
.TP
.B help
Print a summary of the commands.
.TP
.B quit
Leave
.IR qt .
.SH EXAMPLES
Store and read back a counter:
.IP
.EX
: set stats 00000001 hits 0
: incr stats 00000001 hits
1
: get stats 00000001 hits
1
.EE
.PP
Names of the first ten adults whose key starts with
.BR a :
.IP
.EX
: query users prefix a where age >= 18 select name limit 10
.EE
.PP
Rename only if the name is unchanged:
.IP
.EX
: begin
batch 0: if users a3f10c2b name = "ann"
batch 1: set users a3f10c2b name "anna"
batch 2: commit
.EE
.PP
Average age of users that have a status:
.IP
.EX
: avg users age where status exists
.EE
.PP
Load a scratch store from a script:
.IP
.EX
qt -m <<EOF
set users ann name "Ann A" age 31 joined 2026-11-26T10:00:00Z
set users bob name Bob age 17
count users where age >= 18
EOF
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/client
.br
.B internal/mustang
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR quicktable (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success,
1 if the store cannot be opened or a script command fails,
reported as
.BI "qt: line" " n" ": " message\fR,
and 2 on a usage error.
.SH BUGS
The store cannot be shared:
.I qt
cannot open a directory a running server or another
.I qt
holds.
.PP
A column named after a clause cannot follow
.BR select .
